# tools/fora_apply_witness.rish — the committed log drives a state machine, each request applied exactly once (FORA19).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season D thread (Kresfa & Mycelium — the tree's own consensus) · waymark FORA · rung
# FORA19. Design read: active-designing/20260814-083900_fora19-constel-apply-linearizable-sessions.md
#
# constel/apply.rye turns FORA18's committed prefix into applied state, with linearizable client sessions — the guarantee Raft
# §8 names: an agreed log is not a service until each REQUEST runs exactly once. A leader can commit a request, crash before
# answering, and let the client's resend be committed too — the command lands in the log twice. A naive machine applies it twice
# (a transfer of 40 becomes 80); the safe rule remembers, per client, the highest seq it applied and the result, so a retry
# returns the cached answer and mutates nothing. The selftest proves: the machine applies a committed prefix in order; two
# machines fed the same prefix reach a byte-identical state (the replicated-state-machine guarantee); THE §8 REFUSAL — a
# duplicated request applies once; the answer is stable across a retry; only the committed prefix drives the machine and apply is
# incremental; the session table and register are bounded, an unknown client and a register overflow each refusing by name.
# Purely local — no socket, no network, no keys, no funds, no real client, no real address ever formed.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/fora_apply_witness.rish

say "fora-apply: Language EN — FORA19 answers what an agreed log DOES: it drives a replicated state machine, and each client request runs exactly once. Consensus makes the log agreed; it does not make a request run once — that is a separate, provable property (Raft §8), and this rung proves it."
say "fora-apply: Style Radiant — an agreed log is not yet a service; the one rule that makes a retry harmless — remember the highest seq per client and its answer — stays small enough to hold in a single reading."
say "fora-apply: Lens TAME — the Machine's session table is bounded to max_clients and the register to max_register; apply_through folds only FORA18's committed prefix so an uncommitted tail never touches the state; an unknown client, a register overflow, and an over-long commit each refuse by name; siloed to constel/, run from inside the jailed pier; no socket, no network, no keys, no funds, no real client, no real address ever formed (custody gate %2 and gate %4 untouched)."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "constel/bin/apply"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p constel/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build constel/apply.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "fora-apply: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "constel/bin/apply.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "fora-apply: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "fora-apply: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN fora-apply' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "fora-apply: selftest did not print the GREEN fora-apply line"

say "fora-apply: GREEN — the committed log drives a state machine, each request applied exactly once; the constel consensus track now turns an agreed log into a linearizable service, the §8 duplicate-request trap refused, proven pure on the bench."
